# {{ .Vars.wireframes_dir }}/ — what interface work was agreed to look like

One HTML file per screen or state, linked from the spec that introduced it. Layout and
flow only: no colour, no final copy, no behaviour.

```sh
ilk html-wireframe new "Checkout — payment step"
```

Then link it from the spec's `## Wireframe` section. Any spec with `ui: true` needs one,
and `ilk check` says so until it has one.

## Why HTML

Because anybody can open it, in any browser, from disk, with no licence and no account —
including whoever inherits this repository after everyone who drew these has gone. And
because a diff can show what changed about it, which no image format manages.

Each file is self-contained: styles in a `<style>` block, no scripts, nothing fetched
over the network. A sketch that renders as blank boxes when the CDN moves is not a record
of anything.

## Cheap to throw away, on purpose

The scaffold is greyscale boxes in one system font, and it should stay that way. A
wireframe that took three hours is one people defend instead of arguing with — which is
the exact opposite of what it is for. The disagreement is coming either way; all this
does is move it to the day it costs an afternoon rather than a build.

Draw the empty state, the error state and the too-much state. The main state is the one
everybody already imagines the same way, so it teaches you the least.

## Afterwards

Wireframes stay. This is what the work was agreed to be; if the `visual-qa` layer is
present, its screenshots are what it turned out to be. Where the built thing deliberately
differs, say so in the spec — an unexplained difference reads as a mistake to the next
person, and they will go and "fix" it.
